//
// # Why two interfaces rather than one
//
// "Watching the picture" and "changing parameters" have opposite resource semantics: the former is **shared by many**: navigation, recording and AI
// detection can all watch the same stream at once; the latter **must be exclusive**: with two processes changing exposure at once, who wins
// depends on timing, and the picture jumps between the two values.
//
// The kernel issues leases **per resource**, and an endpoint binds one resource. Putting both into one interface
// would make SetExposure request a lease on "a resource this endpoint isn't bound to";
// that path doesn't exist in the kernel, and forcing it would only create a back door around the exclusive semantics.
//
// Split apart, an App that only wants to watch the picture never needs to touch leases, and so is never refused
// because someone else is adjusting parameters.
//
// # Frames don't travel over this protocol
//
// The control plane only **connects the pipe**: OpenStream returns a TransferHandle, the caller uses it
// to attach to the data plane UDS, and then frames flow through the shared memory ring with nervud not on the data path.
//
// **There's deliberately no FrameReady event.** The ring has its own eventfd wake-up, and pushing a "new frame" on the control plane too
// would move once-per-frame traffic back onto the control plane, which is exactly why the shared memory data plane exists. Control plane
// events are kept for things that don't happen per frame: the stream state changed, the device went away.
//
